  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.091,
            0.516,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What you have learnt"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.131,
            0.868,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ability of the eye to focus on both near and distant objects, by adjusting its focal length, is called the accommodation of the eye.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.166,
            0.869,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The smallest distance, at which the eye can see objects clearly without strain, is "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "called the near point of the eye or the least distance of distinct vision. For a young adult with normal vision, it is about 25 cm.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.216,
            0.869,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The common refractive defects of vision include myopia, hypermetropia and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "presbyopia. Myopia (short-sightedness – the image of distant objects is focussed before the retina) is corrected by using a concave lens of suitable power. Hypermetropia (far-sightedness – the image of nearby objects is focussed beyond the retina) is corrected by using a convex lens of suitable power. The eye loses its power of accommodation at old age.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.314,
            0.804,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The splitting of white light into its component colours is called dispersion.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.332,
            0.583,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Scattering of light causes the blue colour of sky.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.381,
            0.588,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "E X E R C I S E S"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.42,
            0.868,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. The human eye can focus on objects at different distances by adjusting the focal length of the eye lens. This is due to (a) presbyopia. (b) accommodation. (c) near-sightedness. (d) far-sightedness.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.501,
            0.788,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. The human eye forms the image of an object at its (a) cornea. (b) iris. (c) pupil. (d) retina.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.54,
            0.866,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. The least distance of distinct vision for a young adult with normal vision is about (a) 25 m. (b) 2.5 cm. (c) 25 cm. (d) 2.5 m.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.58,
            0.834,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. The change in focal length of an eye lens is caused by the action of the (a) pupil. (b) retina. (c) ciliary muscles. (d) iris.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.62,
            0.868,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. A person needs a lens of power –5.5 dioptres for correcting his distant vision. For correcting his near vision he needs a lens of power +1.5 dioptre. What is the focal length of the lens required for correcting (i) distant vision, and (ii) near vision?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.672,
            0.868,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. The far point of a myopic person is 80 cm in front of the eye. What is the nature and power of the lens required to correct the problem?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.708,
            0.868,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Make a diagram to show how hypermetropia is corrected. The near point of a hypermetropic eye is 1 m. What is the power of the lens required to correct this defect? Assume that the near point of the normal eye is 25 cm.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.759,
            0.865,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Why is a normal eye not able to see clearly the objects placed closer than 25 cm?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.78,
            0.868,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. What happens to the image distance in the eye when we increase the distance of an object from the eye?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.816,
            0.357,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10. Why do stars twinkle?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.836,
            0.506,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11. Explain why the planets do not twinkle.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.856,
            0.714,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12. Why does the sky appear dark instead of blue to an astronaut?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.089,
            0.917,
            0.124,
